Lattice-based cryptography—specifically Learning With Errors (LWE) and Module-LWE—offers robust theoretical security guarantees grounded in the hardness of geometric high-dimensional vector space problems (such as the Shortest Vector Problem, SVP).

```rust
// Rust verification stub for polynomial ring multiplication
pub fn verify_lattice_polynomial(a: &[i32; 256], s: &[i32; 256], e: &[i32; 256]) -> [i32; 256] {
    let mut result = ntt_multiply(a, s);
    vector_add(&mut result, e);
    result
}
```

NIST standards such as ML-KEM (Kyber) and ML-DSA (Dilithium) form the bedrock of our verified communication pipelines.

WebAssembly delivers linear, deterministic, and sandboxed memory spaces bounded by compiler guarantees. By running native Rust microkernels compiled directly to WASM bytecode, host workers can instantiate thousands of isolated tenant runtimes within a single unified process space.

```rust
// WebAssembly host runtime instantiation
let engine = Engine::default();
let module = Module::from_file(&engine, "service.wasm")?;
let mut store = Store::new(&engine, ());
let instance = Instance::new(&mut store, &module, &[])?;
```

Cold-start latencies plummet from 800ms down to sub-50 microseconds, allowing true scale-to-zero microservices.

Neuromorphic event cameras mimic biological photoreceptors. Each pixel operates independently and asynchronously, firing an event tuple (x, y, timestamp, polarity) only when local log-intensity changes exceed a programmable threshold.

```cpp
// Asynchronous event packet dispatch
struct Event {
    uint16_t x;
    uint16_t y;
    uint64_t timestamp_us;
    int8_t polarity;
};
```

This delivers microsecond-level temporal resolution (>10,000 equivalent FPS) and over 140dB dynamic range without exposure saturation.
